    "lawCode": "Amd §667, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2295               REVISED 03/26/10\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to tuition assistance\nprogram awards\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide greater tuition assistance\nprogram (TAP) awards to students who are considered independent due to\nrelinquished parental control.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection one of the bill amends clause (A) of subparagraph (i) of\nparagraph a of subdivision 3 of section 667 of the education law,\nconcerning the undergraduate dependent TAP award amounts, to also\ninclude those students who are considered independent due to an\nexisting condition wherein parental control has been relinquished.\n\nSection two of the bill amends subparagraph (iii) of paragraph a of\nsubdivision 3 of section 667 of the education law to provide that\nundergraduate independent TAP award amounts include those students who\nhave been granted an exclusion of parental income solely due to\nemancipation.\n\nSection three amends subparagraph (iii) of paragraph b of subdivision\n3 of section 667 of the education law to state that two-year business\nschool independent TAP award amounts include those students who have\nbeen granted an exclusion of parental income solely due to\nemancipation.\n\nSection four of the bill amends subparagraph (iii) of paragraph c of\nsubdivision 3 of section 667 of the education law to provide that\ngraduate school TAP award amounts include those students who have been\ngranted an exclusion of parental income solely due to emancipation.\n\nSection five is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nUnder current law, \"independent students\" are either students who have\nbeen determined to be emancipated or students whose parents have\nrelinquished parental control. In order to determine whether parental\ncontrol has been relinquished, the president of the higher education\nservice corporation and the director of the division of budget\nestablish criteria to recognize unusual and exceptional family\ncircumstances in which parents no longer provide care or financial\nsupport to students under age twenty-two. Currently this criteria\nincludes the following circumstances: parents are deceased; parents\nare declared incompetent by judicial action or certified as having a\ntotal or permanent disability; the student is a ward of the court; the\nstudent receives public assistance (other than food stamps, aid as a\ndependent child or unemployment); and the student is rendered\nfinancially independent due to the involuntary dissolution of the\nfamily.\n\nFor purposes of TAP, independent students are subject to a different\nincome schedule than dependent students which routinely allows them to\nbecome eligible for less TAP money. Where parental control has been\nrelinquished, students, through no fault of their own, face difficult\ncircumstances which may include removal from the home due to neglect\nor abuse, entering foster care, and/or becoming a ward of the court.\nIt seems only fair to place these students on a schedule that would\ntake into account their exceptional family circumstances and convey\nmore funds.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008 - A.9692 Passed the Assembly\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nA fiscal note dated 3/18/2010 prepared by examiner Steve Hallock\nstates: The 2010-11 Executive Budget includes an Article VII proposal\nwhich would increase maximum TAP awards for this same group of\nindividuals from $3,025 to $5,000 while increasing the income range\nused to determine the applicant's TAP award to $25,000. The estimated\ncost of these provisions is $3.87 million in 2010-11 and $5.529\nmillion annually thereafter.\n\nEnactment of this bill will produce an incremental cost of $16,800 in\naddition to the Executive Budget proposal in 2010-11. When fully\nannualized, this bill would result in an increased cost of $24,000\nabove the annualized value of the 2010-11 Executive Budget proposal.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
